LOGIN(1)                       P32W Unix                        LOGIN(1)

NAME
       login - sign on to the system

SYNOPSIS
       login [name [tty]]

DESCRIPTION
       login checks a user's password and starts that user's shell.
       It is normally run by getty(8), which opens the terminal,
       prints the banner and the login: prompt, and executes
       /bin/login name ttyN with the name typed.

       Without a name, login asks for one itself (login: ). tty is
       the terminal's name without /dev/ (tty0 or tty1); it defaults
       to tty0.

       If the account has a password, login turns echo off and asks
       for it (Password: ). An unknown name is asked for a password
       too, so a caller cannot tell the two failures apart. The
       password is checked with the library's salted hash (see
       passwd(5)); a password field starting with * never matches,
       which locks the account, and an empty field needs no
       password at all. On failure login prints Login incorrect,
       waits (2 seconds, then 3, then 4) and asks for the name
       again; after the third failure it exits, and init(8) starts
       a new getty on the terminal. An empty name is simply asked
       for again and does not count as a try.

       On success login:

       -  gives the terminal /dev/ttyN to the user: owner the
          user, group tty, mode 0620 (write(1) and wall(1) are
          setgid tty; mesg(1) takes the group's write away);

       -  writes the terminal's record in /etc/utmp (name, terminal,
          time), which who(1) and uptime(1) read (only for a
          terminal named ttyN: the digit picks the record);

       -  changes to the home directory (if it is missing, it says
          No directory dir: logging in with home / and uses /);

       -  sets the group id and the user id;

       -  copies /etc/motd to the terminal, and says You have
          mail. if /usr/spool/mail/name is not empty;

       -  executes the user's shell (/bin/sh if the field is empty)
          with argv[0] set to the shell's name with a leading -
          (as -sh), the traditional mark of a login shell. The
          environment is replaced by exactly:

              PATH=/bin:/usr/bin
              HOME=the home directory
              USER=the name
              LOGNAME=the name
              SHELL=the shell

       When the shell exits, init(8) clears the utmp record and
       starts a new getty on that terminal.

EXAMPLES
       P32W Unix (tty1)

       login: ken
       Password:
       Welcome to P32W Unix.
       $

FILES
       /etc/passwd    names, password hashes, ids, homes, shells
       /etc/utmp      who is logged in, one record per terminal
       /etc/motd      the message of the day
       /usr/spool/mail/name
                      the user's mailbox (mail(1))
       /dev/tty0, /dev/tty1
                      the terminals

EXIT STATUS
       Only on failure (otherwise login becomes the shell): 1 for
       a wrong name or password, a failed setuid, or a shell that
       cannot be executed.

SEE ALSO
       sh(1), passwd(1), su(1), who(1), getty(8), init(8),
       passwd(5), utmp(5), motd(5), mesg(1), mail(1)

BUGS
       Nothing is logged about failed attempts.

       TZ is not passed on; programs fall back on /etc/TZ (see
       tz(5)).

       Names and passwords longer than 62 characters are cut short.
